  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThrough Saturday: Record high temperatures along with higher \nhumidity and dry weather is expected. Latest satellite imagery/RAP \nanalysis showed the circulation around a large 596dam high centered \nover the ArkLaTex region. This large high will remain in place and \nweaken some through Saturday. This will allow moisture topping the \nhigh to spill over across northern Mississippi and to our east. The \ndrier air from the east that has helped hold humidity down the past \nfew days will shift west of our CWA as moisture increases from the \nnortheast Saturday. This increase in moisture will combine with \nrecord high temperatures that will result in peak heat index values \nbetween 105-1108F in our northeast and east Mississippi counties \nwith lower heat index values approaching 105F elsewhere. The \nincrease in moisture will lead to some convection just to our \nnortheast but no measurable precipitation is anticipated in our \nCWA. /22/ \n\nThe anomalous ridge will continue to promote hot and dry conditions \ninto early next week. The streak of potentially record breaking high \ntemperatures, especially north of I-20 will continue at least \nthrough Monday, possibly Tuesday. Elevated heat stress continues to \nbe advertised west of I-55 for early next week. Rain will remain \nscarce, but a few popup showers cannot be ruled out, mostly east of \nI-55.\n\nBy the middle of next week, a digging trough to the north and an \nupper low over Florida work in tandem to break the ridge down, \nrelieving heat stress.  This also means there will be a low chance \nfor a few rain showers with our area situated beneath the \nbase/through axis of an deepening upper low to the north. Even so, \ntemperatures are likely to remain above average. Towards late next \nweek, there is a bit of divergence in guidance with 2 scenarios. One \nscenario is the aforementioned trough digs enough to push a trailing \nfrontal boundary into the area, which leads to less hot conditions. \nThe other, reinforces ridging, stalling the frontal boundary just \nnorth of the area. This would probably mean slightly warmer \ntemperatures. Guidance will likely converge and come into agreement \nas we get closer. /SAS/",
